.TH "tangsible-vault" "1" "2026-11-25" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
tangsible-vault - edit individually vault-encrypted variables in place
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBtangsible vault\fR [--vault-password-file \fIpath\fR | --ask-vault-pass] [--vault-id \fIid@source\fR].\&.\&.\& [--encrypt-vault-id \fIlabel\fR] \fIfilename\fR
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBvault\fR provides an \fBansible-vault edit\fR-like experience, but scoped to
individual variables inside the file instead of the whole file.\&
.PP
\fIfilename\fR is decrypted in place: every top-level \fB!\&vault\fR-tagged value
becomes its plain decrypted string, opened in the editor.\& On save, only
values that actually changed are re-encrypted; everything else — comments,
key order, previously unencrypted values, and any untouched ciphertext — is
spliced back byte-for-byte from the original file, not regenerated.\&
.PP
If \fIfilename\fR does not exist, it is created empty.\& An empty file is a file
with no variables: the editor opens on it, and every variable added there is
encrypted on save.\&
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fB--vault-password-file\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 4
Read the vault password from \fIpath\fR (a file, or an executable that
prints it).\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--ask-vault-pass\fR
.RS 4
Prompt for the vault password.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--vault-id\fR \fIid@source\fR
.RS 4
A password and the vault id (label) it belongs to; the source is a file,
an executable, or \fBprompt\fR.\& Without \fBid@\fR the id is \fBdefault\fR.\&
May be repeated.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB--encrypt-vault-id\fR \fIlabel\fR
.RS 4
The vault id a new value is encrypted with.\&
.PP
.RE
With none of these, the password comes from \fB$ANSIBLE_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE\fR,
then \fBvault_password_file\fR in \fBansible.\&cfg\fR, then a prompt.\& The vault ids
configured in \fB$ANSIBLE_VAULT_IDENTITY_LIST\fR or \fBvault_identity_list\fR in
\fBansible.\&cfg\fR are used first, then every \fB--vault-id\fR.\& The id for new
values defaults to \fB$ANSIBLE_VAULT_ENCRYPT_IDENTITY\fR or
\fBvault_encrypt_identity\fR.\&
.PP
.SH VAULT IDS
.PP
Values may be encrypted under different vault ids (\fB$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.\&2;AES256;label\fR),
each with its own password.\& Every value is decrypted with the password whose
id matches the label in its header first, then with any other password you
gave, as Ansible does: a label is a hint, any password that decrypts the
value works.\&
.PP
A changed value is encrypted again with the id and password it had.\& A new
value uses \fB--encrypt-vault-id\fR; without one, the vault id all of the file'\&s
values share if you gave its password, otherwise the first password.\&
.PP
A value that none of the given passwords decrypts (another team'\&s, say) is
shown as \fB<vaulted: label>\fR and saved unchanged as long as the placeholder is
left alone.\& Changing it is refused; deleting the line removes the value.\& This
also holds when no value at all can be decrypted (say a file whose values all
use another vault id): the file opens with placeholders only, and a value
added is encrypted with the id given by \fB--encrypt-vault-id\fR, else the first
password.\&
.PP
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
The file was saved successfully, or the editor closed with no
changes at all (in which case nothing was written).\&
.PP
.RE
\fB1\fR
.RS 4
A genuine failure: the file couldn'\&t be read or decrypted (wrong
password, corrupted data), or some other I/O error.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB2\fR
.RS 4
Usage error: no \fIfilename\fR was given, both password flags were
given together, or \fB--encrypt-vault-id\fR names an id no password was
given for.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB130\fR
.RS 4
The session was reverted — either explicitly, via the fix-or-revert
prompt, or by Ctrl-C reaching \fBtangsible\fR directly (only possible
outside an active editor session: before it first opens, or between
reopen rounds).\& The target file is left exactly as it was.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.PP
Only top-level values of a flat YAML mapping are handled, and not a file
that is encrypted as a whole.\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBtangsible\fR(1), \fBansible-vault\fR(1)
